  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 342 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nThrough tonight: Latest surface analysis had a nearly stationary \ncold front along the Natchez Trace. This frontal boundary will \nremain in place through tonight and serve as the focus for \nincreasing rain chances. There was already a layer of stratus \ndeveloping over the CWA that will limit insolation this morning but \nheating by this afternoon will lead to convection. The greatest rain \nchances and rainfall amounts are expected along and south of the \nfrontal boundary. This is expected to be beneficial rainfall through \ntonight. /22/ \n\nSunday through next weekend...\n\nMoist airmass remains locked in over the area in advance of a slowly \nsagging frontal boundary, which will keep high (80%) rain chances. \nMost of the steady precip will remain along and south of the Natchez \nTrace corridor. This area appears to be stabilized by ongoing rain \nand stratus, thus limiting convective rates. Along the leading edge, \nthere is potential for deeper convection, convective rates and \nheavier rainfall, as well as lightning, roughly along the Natchez \ntrace corridor. This band of convective showers should propagate \neastward through Sunday afternoon. Trailing stratiform rain will be \nslow to taper, persisting through much of the overnight hours into \nearly monday morning. \n\nAs the front begins to make headway into the area Monday, rain will \ncome to an end as dry air pushes in its wake. 50s Dewpoints in the \nupper will allow lows to cool into the middle 50s in the north and \nlow 60s in the south. There is a bit of crossover Monday night, \nespecially in the southeast, so fog/low stratus is a possibility.\n\nThe remainder of the week will be dry, reinforced by NW flow in the \nwake of the trough. Temperatures will be more seasonal with highs in \nthe 80s and lows in the 60s. Rain chances return next weekend as a \nshortwave out of Texas/western Gulf will return the moist airmass \nwith 2+ inch PWAT. Although there is some flow with this shortwave, \nlapse rates are rather moist adiabatic, thus insufficient for \nsevere. There may be enough support however to necessitate \nmonitoring for heavy rainfall as we get closer. /SAS/",
